{"input_text": "Question: I have this sequence and I'm trying to calculate its limit 
 $$\prod_{k=n+1}^{k=2n} (1-\tfrac{1}{k^2})$$
anyone have an idea how can I solve this?
thanks for the help 

Answer: $\newcommand{\bbx}[1]{\,\bbox[15px,border:1px groove navy]{\displaystyle{#1}}\,}
 \newcommand{\braces}[1]{\left\lbrace\,{#1}\,\right\rbrace}
 \newcommand{\bracks}[1]{\left\lbrack\,{#1}\,\right\rbrack}
 \newcommand{\dd}{\mathrm{d}}
 \newcommand{\ds}[1]{\displaystyle{#1}}
 \newcommand{\expo}[1]{\,\mathrm{e}^{#1}\,}
 \newcommand{\ic}{\mathrm{i}}
 \newcommand{\mc}[1]{\mathcal{#1}}
 \newcommand{\mrm}[1]{\mathrm{#1}}
 \newcommand{\pars}[1]{\left(\,{#1}\,\right)}
 \newcommand{\partiald}[3][]{\frac{\partial^{#1} #2}{\partial #3^{#1}}}
 \newcommand{\root}[2][]{\,\sqrt[#1]{\,{#2}\,}\,}
 \newcommand{\totald}[3][]{\frac{\mathrm{d}^{#1} #2}{\mathrm{d} #3^{#1}}}
 \newcommand{\verts}[1]{\left\vert\,{#1}\,\right\vert}$
\begin{align}
\prod_{k\ =\ n + 1}^{k\ =\ 2n}\pars{1 - {1 \over k^{2}}} & =
{\bracks{\prod_{k\ =\ n + 1}^{k\ =\ 2n}\pars{k - 1}}
\bracks{\prod_{k\ =\ n + 1}^{k\ =\ 2n}\pars{k + 1}} \over
\pars{\prod_{k\ =\ n + 1}^{k\ =\ 2n}k}^{2}} =
{n^{\large\overline{n}}\pars{n + 2}^{\large\overline{n}} \over
\bracks{\pars{n + 1}^{\large\overline{n}}}^{2}}
\label{1}\tag{1}
\\[5mm] & =
{\bracks{\Gamma\pars{2n}/\Gamma\pars{n}}
\bracks{\Gamma\pars{2n + 2}/\Gamma\pars{n + 2}} \over
\bracks{\Gamma\pars{2n + 1}/\Gamma\pars{n + 1}}^{\,2}}
\\[5mm] & =
{\Gamma\pars{2n}\Gamma\pars{2n + 2}\Gamma^{2}\pars{n + 1} \over
\Gamma\pars{n}\Gamma\pars{n + 2}\Gamma^{2}\pars{2n + 1}}
=
{\bracks{\pars{2n}!/\pars{2n}}\bracks{2n\pars{2n}!}\pars{n!}^{2} \over
\bracks{n!/n}\bracks{\pars{n + 1}n!}\bracks{\pars{2n}!}^{\,2}}
\\[5mm] & =
{n \over n + 1}\,\,\,\stackrel{\mrm{as}\ n\ \to\ \infty}{\to}\,\,\,
\bbx{1}
\end{align} 

 Indeed, the task simplest way is the 'telescoping manipulation' of second expression in \eqref{1}. 

