{"input_text": "Question: We have that $X\sim U[0,2]$ and $Y=\min (X,1)$.  
 I want to find the distribution of $Y$.  
 We have that \begin{align*}F_Y(y)&=P(Y\leq y)=P(\min (X,1)\leq y)=P([X\leq y] \text{ or } \ [1\leq y])=P(\{X\leq y\}\cup \{1\leq y\})\\ & =P(X\leq y)+P(1\leq y)\end{align*}  
 Is everything correct so far?  
 Since $X\sim U[0,2]$ do we have that $P(X\leq y)=y$ ? What does it hold for $P(1\leq y)$ ?  
 $$$$  
 Coul you give me also a hint how we could calculate the expected value of $Y$ ?  

Answer: The given differential equation  
 \begin{align}
& \dfrac{dy}{dt} = 30 - \dfrac{20y}{800 + 30 t} = 30 - \dfrac{2y}{80 + 3 t} \\
\implies & \dfrac{dy}{dt} + \dfrac{2}{80 + 3 t} \, y = 30 \\
\implies & \dfrac{dy}{dt} + P y = Q. \tag{1}
\end{align}
where $P = \frac{2}{80 + 3 t}$ and $Q = 30$. 
 The integration factor $I$ can be calculated as 
\begin{align}
 I & = \exp \left[ \int P \, dt \right] = \exp \left[ \int \dfrac{2}{80 + 3 t} \, dt\right] \\
& = \exp \left[ \dfrac{2}{3} \log (80 + 3t)\right] = (80 + 3t)^{2/3}. \tag{2}
\end{align} 
 Multiplying (1) by the integration factor yields, 
\begin{align}
& (80 + 3t)^{2/3} \dfrac{dy}{dt} + \dfrac{2}{80 + 3t} (80 + 3t)^{2/3} y = 30 (80 + 3t)^{2/3} \\
\implies & (80 + 3t)^{2/3} \dfrac{dy}{dt} + 2 (80 + 3t)^{-1/3} y = 30 (80 + 3t)^{2/3}. \tag{3}
\end{align} 
 Integrating (3) yields, 
\begin{align}
& (80 + 3t)^{2/3} y = 6(80 + 3t)^{5/3} + C \\
\implies & y = 6 \times (80 + 3t) + C (80 + 3t)^{-2/3} \\
\implies & \boxed{y = C (80 + 3t)^{-2/3} + 18 t + 480}
