{"input_text": "Question: I'm giving the simultaneous density function:
\begin{equation*}
f(x,y)= \begin{cases}
\frac{4x^3}{y^3} & \text{if $0<x<1, x<y$}\\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
Then I'm told to calculate $P(X+Y\leq1)$.
I've been struggling for couple of hours with the calculations, which I think is supposed to be:
\begin{equation*}
P(X+Y\leq 1)=\int_{0}^{1/2}\int_x^{1-x}\frac{4x^3}{y^3}\,\mathrm{d}y\,\mathrm{d}x
\end{equation*}
But the problem here is that I get this:
\begin{equation*}
\int_{0}^{1/2}-\frac{2x^3}{(1-x)^2}+2x\,\mathrm{d}y\,\mathrm{d}x
\end{equation*}
and don't know how to continue since it gets worse for every partial integration I take.  
 Any help will be greatly appreciated. Thanks!
By the way I have to do it by hand, which is why it's difficult. 

Answer: The trick is to do some operations both before  and after  applying the induction hypothesis. 
 The starting case for induction would be $n=1$, in which case there are no operations needed (or possible), and the unique entry of the matrix equals its determinant. 
 In the induction step $n>1$, and then you can apply row operations to make the first column equal to that of the identity matrix. This requires a bit of work, but you claim to have done this. Note that development of the determinant of the modified matrix by the first column, which should give the value $\pm\det A$ and can be arranged to give ${+}\det A$, results in the determinant of the lower right ḃlock $B$ of size $(n-1)\times(n-1)$; in particular this determinant is nonzero and $B\in GL_{n-1}(K)$. 
 Now by the induction hypothesis, there is a sequence of row operations that transforms $B$ into its corresponding diagonal with lower-right entry $\det(B)=\det(A)$. This same sequence can be applied to the last $n-1$ rows of the matrix from which $B$ was extracted, which rows have an additional leading$~0$, but these entries are unaffected by the row operations. The result is a matrix with the main diagonal and its last $n-1$ lines as they should be, but with uncontrolled entries in the first row, after the first column. Now simply apply row operations from each of the final $n-1$ rows towards the first row to clear these uncontrolled entries out (since $\det A$ is invertible, there is no problem to do this in the final column.) 
